\par\par \b \uc2  Exotic  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Focus: India Rupee Seen Calmer After Fall \b0\par\par\uc2  By Pankaj Aher \par \uc2 318 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 1997\par 04:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (AP-Dow Jones)--A swift response by monetary officials to a recent fall in the Indian rupee and continuing foreign investment inflows are likely to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steady in a relatively narrow range over the coming months, economists and analysts say. \par \par \uc2  After the rupee fell to an 18-month low of 36.72 rupees against the U.S. dollar Sept. 9, down nearly 3% from mid-August, India's Finance Ministry moved rapidly to talk up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to steady the market. \par \par \uc2  Analysts now say the aggressive action has helped to damp concerns that the rupee could be hit by the same speculative selling that has hit Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks. Foreign investment continuing to flow into the stock market will also help the rupee hold its ground for the near future. \par \par \uc2  Traders and analysts say they expect the rupee to trade in a range between 36.35 rupees and 37.25 rupees to the dollar for the next few months because the fundamentals are strong and foreign inflows are expected to continue. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of India is also expected to remain vigilant, effectively preventing any further sharp and sudden depreciation of the rupee. \par \par \uc2  Abheek Barua, economist at S.S. Kantilal Inshwarlal Securities Ltd., says the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to buy dollars if it falls to around 36.35 rupees, so that the rupee doesn't strengthen too much and hurt export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  But analysts also note that the the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to sell dollars if any major rupee weakness emerges, preventing any climb by the dollar to 37.25 rupees. \par \par \uc2  'A two-way movement in the rupee is more likely than a gradual depreciation,' Barua of SSKI says. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 18-09-97 \par \par \uc2  0200GMT \par \par \uc2  1106 1838 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt9i06vc1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
