\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee Slightly Weaker Against Dlr; Early Dlr Demand \b0\par\par\uc2 304 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 1997\par 05:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee opened slightly weaker against the dollar Thursday and slowly fell further because of some dollar demand from banks, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened Thursday between 36.54 rupees and 36.57 rupees, from its Wednesday close of between 36.5250 rupees and 36.5450 rupees. \par \par \uc2  At 0340 GMT (11:40 p.m. EDT), the dollar is trading between 36.56 rupees and 36.59 rupees. \par \par \uc2  'There is some demand for the dollar from banks because of expectation that the State Bank of India, the country's largest commercial bank, will continue to buy dollars,' a dealer at a U.S. bank said. \par \par \uc2  As reported, the State Bank most likely bought dollars Wednesday estimated at $100 million largely for oil companies, to meet their imports and loan repayments. \par \par \uc2  State Bank is the largest participant in the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on behalf of state-owned and large private companies, mainly buying dollars on behalf of the major oil companies, for their imports. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said other than the State Bank purchases there isn't expected to be any major buying from other importers and companies. \par \par \uc2  They said that they expected the dollar to range between 36.53 rupees and 36.60 rupees for the rest of the trading session Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The rupee had recovered significantly Wednesday and banks offered dollars at a low of around 36.43 rupees but later the dollar gained slightly on State Bank purchases. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is expected to be less volatile after the Reserve Bank of India Governor and India's Finance Minister Wednesday morning said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a orderly \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES \b \highlight22\uc2 INDIA\b0 \highlight\uc2  REPORT  11-09-97 \par \par \uc2  0357GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 1847 62506 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020011004dt9b018ln\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
