\par\par \b \uc2  India Govt Vows Stable Rupee, But Notes Economy Worrying \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vikash Jain \par \uc2 247 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1997\par 15:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI (AP-Dow Jones)--Dizzied by the Indian rupee's roller-coaster ride during the last three weeks, the government Wednesday sought order, claiming that solid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and generally good economic fundamentals should damp rupee volatility. \par \par \uc2  The rupee dipped to an 18-month low of 36.72 rupees to the dollar this week despite heavy dollar-selling by the Reserve Bank of India to prop up the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Comments by key officials Wednesday brought on a rebound, but some analysts question its sustainability. \par \par \uc2  'What we want is orderly conditions in the market,' Finance Minister P. Chidambaram said Wednesday at a conference for economic editors. He noted that India had a much smaller current account deficit - about 1.3% of gross domestic product - than many Southeast Asian countries hit by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation in the past month. \par \par \uc2  And earlier Wednesday, RBI Governor C. Rangarajan issued a statement to the market saying 'the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to reduce volatility and to ensure orderly conditions.' \par \par \uc2  But some economists think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  are sending mixed signals to the market because both have also recently noted signs of a widening budget deficit, a possible upswing in inflation, high interest rates, and diminishing growth prospects because the country's poor infrastructure is holding development back. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 10-09-97 \par \par \uc2  1342GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt9a06nj4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
