\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee Premiums On Fwd Dlrs Sharply Dn; All-Round Sales \b0\par\par\uc2 342 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1997\par 08:44\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee premiums for forward dollars Wednesday fell sharply on all-round selling of the spot and the forward dollars by banks and importers because of the finance minister's statement that the government wants orderly conditions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  'It is a mad rush with banks unwinding their long spot and forward dollar positions and importers who had earlier rushed to cover their future dollar requirements at higher premiums canceling their contracts,' a dealer at a U.S. bank said. \par \par \uc2  The premiums for forward dollars to be delivered at the end of March is between 1.29 rupees and 1.33 rupees, lower from its Tuesday close of between 1.50 rupees and 1.53 rupees. The premiums on the forward dollar are lower at an annualized 6.50% from its recent high of about 8.50%. \par \par \uc2  Banks started selling the spot and the forward dollars following comments by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to reduce volatility, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  'The fall in the spot-dollar and the forward premiums was sharper because of the finance minister reiterating the RBI governors statement on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market,' said a dealer at a state-owned bank. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister, P. Chidambaram earlier said that the government wants orderly conditions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and that the government hasn't yet taken a decision on the trading band for the rupee against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  At 0630 GMT (2:30 a.m. EDT), the dollar is trading sharply lower from its opening levels of between 36.42 rupees and 36.44 rupees. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened Wednesday between 36.58 rupees and 36.61 rupees, from its Tuesday close of between 36.59 rupees and 36.61 rupees. \par \par \uc2  For the latest rupee indicative rates, please see Dow Jones Markets page [17960]. \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES INDIA REPORT  10-09-97 \par \par \uc2  0644GMT \par \par \uc2  62506 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020011004dt9a018ak\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
