\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-India says won't tolerate rupee volatility. \b0\par\par\uc2 595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1997\par 09:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI, Sept 10 (Reuter) - Indian financial authorities led by Finance Minister P. Chidambaram and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor on Wednesday threw their weight against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators and vowed to defend the rupee. \par \par \uc2  Their words appeared to have the desired effect. On Wednesday the rupee rose smartly to around 36.475/495 to the dollar at 0730 GMT after closing on Tuesday at 36.59/61. \par \par \uc2  On Monday it ended at a 19-month low of 36.685/95 to the dollar, but recovered on strong RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) Governor C. Rangarajan said in a statement issued in Bombay that the country's economic fundamentals were strong and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  both in the spot and forward rupee markets to reduce volatility. \par \par \uc2  "While the exchange rate is market-determined, the RBI will not allow volatility and excessive speculation," Chidambaram told an annual meeting of economic editors in New Delhi. \par \par \uc2  "What we want are orderly conditions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," he said. "We want orderly conditions in the market and let the market determine the exchange rate." \par \par \uc2  Chidambaram said Southeast Asian exchange markets had been extremely volatile. "It is naive to think that some of that volatility has not spilled over. It has spilled over to the Indian market." \par \par \uc2  After trading at a sedate 35.70 to the dollar for several months, the rupee has been hit by major turbulence over the past few weeks. \par \par \uc2  "There has been no basic change in the fundamentals of the economy which continue to remain strong," Rangarajan said. \par \par \uc2  But Chidambaram was not equally optimistic, saying India's fiscal deficit was under severe pressure. \par \par \uc2  "This year we hope for a further fiscal correction but I am afraid there are severe pressures," Chidambaram told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Chidambaram said the government had estimated the 1996/97 (April-March) fiscal deficit at 5.0 percent of gross domestic product (GDP) but added: "It may end up at 5.1 percent." \par \par \uc2  "I think it still has not been inscribed on the consciousness of the Indian political and economic leadership that fiscal correction is the single most important goal we must pursue," Chidambaram said. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday the International Monetary Fund in its annual report urged India to aim for an accelerated reduction in its large fiscal deficit, adding a delay in fiscal adjustment would leave the economy vulnerable to adverse external developments. \par \par \uc2  India aims to reduce its fiscal deficit as a proportion of GDP to 4.5 percent in 1997/98. "The too large fiscal deficit still needed to be tackled decisively," the IMF said. \par \par \uc2  Chidambaram and his chief aide, Finance Secretary Montek Singh Ahluwalia, were at pains to stress that India was unlikely to be affected by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in Southeast Asia because its own economic fundamentals were stronger. \par \par \uc2  "I have said there is some turmoil in the Southeast Asian markets," he said. "I am not of the view that we are affected by the same degree as Southeast Asia." \par \par \uc2  Ahluwalia, the highest-ranking civil servant in the Finance Ministry, said India's current-account deficit as a share of GDP had been hovering between 1.3 and 1.4 percent and noted that in Thailand it was eight percent when that country was hit by the baht crisis. \par \par \uc2  He said Indian banks have reduced their short-term borrowing as opposed to Thai banks which relied heavily on short-term borrowing. "India's situation is hugely different from Thailand's," he said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt9a003p2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
