\par\par \b \uc2  HSBC sees RBI  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  stabilising  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  rupee. \b0\par\par\uc2 323 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Sept 9 (Reuter) - Continued \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will help steady the Indian rupee in the short term but it was expected to depreciate to around 37.5 per dollar by June 1998, HSBC Markets said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "If the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) continues to defend the rupee at 36.50-70 (which we believe it can and will) over the next few weeks, the apprehensions of importers would subside and the demand-supply situation will revert in favour of the rupee," it said in a commentary on the Indian rupee. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday the rupee dropped to 36.72 per dollar within minutes of opening before RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and unwinding of long dollar positions helped it recover to close at 36.59/61. The Indian rupee ended Monday at 36.685/695. \par \par \uc2  "Looking ahead, we expect a depreciation of 4.0-5.0 percent over the next year would find favour with the government and the rupee would move to 37.50 per dollar by June 1998," it said. \par \par \uc2  The commentary said a change in sentiment has triggered the current spell of weakness in the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Importers and borrowers in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have started to hedge their payables," it said. \par \par \uc2  HSBC Markets said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed that there had been no drop in supply of spot dollars from corporates but there had been a pick-up in demand for forward dollars. \par \par \uc2  The Indian rupee has weakened by more than than two percent from mid-August when an RBI official said the rupee appeared overvalued. \par \par \uc2  HSBC Markets said it did not rule out skittishness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the short term. \par \par \uc2  "However volatility in the immediate future cannot be ruled out as both importers and exporters try to find out the right level for the rupee." \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay newsroom (91)-22-265 9000; fax (91)-22-264 1699 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt99001hf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
