\par\par \b \uc2  India RBI piloting rupee to soft landing - dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shyam Kumar \par \uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Sept 9 (Reuter) - The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (RBI) daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market aims to ensure its overvalued rupee depreciates smoothly rather than to turn the tide, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has fallen by over two percent since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  labelled its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  overvalued in mid-August. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a 19-month low of 36.72 per rupee, but rallied after the RBI sold up to $120 million and by 1.30 p.m. (0800 GMT) stood around 36.61 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The rupee had held steady against the dollar at 35.70/85 for nearly a year until the mid-August watershed. \par \par \uc2  But it had appreciated on its real effective exchange rate index -- which takes into account inflation differentials against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- prompting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  concern. \par \par \uc2  However, having given the green light to sell rupees, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had to act to stop a precipitous fall. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said they expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aims to pilot the rupee to around 37 rupees per dollar. \par \par \uc2  "I think RBI is helping a soft-landing to 37 (rupees to a dollar) level," Ravi Kumar, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at ABN-AMRO Bank N.V. in Bombay told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  This week, RBI began selling dollars at 36.65 rupees on Monday before stepping up the price to 36.685 and on Tuesday sold at 36.72. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said many Indian exporters had cancelled forward dollar sales, increasing nervousness among Indian firms needing to cover forward dollar commitments. \par \par \uc2  A horde of Indian firms who raised offshore loans and did not take forward cover as the rupee held steady against the dollar until August have now rushed to cover, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Some commentators have said some speculation by banks was adding to the pressure on the rupee. But many had earlier taken positions expecting the rupee to strengthen. \par \par \uc2  "It is to be understood that players who sold dollars at 35.70/80 levels disregarding the need for correction of the rupee were also speculators," French bank Societe Generale said in an India commentary on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "The same speculators are turning hedgers now to get out of their position. The sudden weakness of the rupee is justified to help exporters to compete better and in no way reflects the state of the economy which is still on the growth path." \par \par \uc2  Indian exporters have been complaining that the overvalued rupee was one of the main reasons for the deceleration in India's export growth in 1997/98 (April-March). \par \par \uc2  "It would appear there is more of it (rupee's depreciation) to come, with the RBI only being able to slow the move," a leading European bank told its clients in a report. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay Newsroom (91) 22 265 9000 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt99001fq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
