\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee Weakens Further Vs Dollar In Midday Trading \b0\par\par\uc2 359 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 1997\par 09:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (AP-Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee is steadily falling against the U.S. dollar Monday despite the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollar forwards, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0650 GMT (2:50 a.m. EDT), the dollar is trading between 36.6750 rupees and 36.6925 rupees. \par \par \uc2  'There is good amount of demand for dollars from importers and large companies because of the bullish sentiment on the dollar,' said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  'With the RBI not aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the dollar, banks are going in for long dollar positions,' he added. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they estimated the RBI to have sold spot-dollars worth about $50 million earlier. \par \par \uc2  They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is continuing to keep its presence felt in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by selling forward dollars for delivery from end-October to end-February to various domestic and foreign banks. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened Monday between 36.58 rupees and 36.60 rupees, little changed from its Friday close of between 36.58 rupees and 36.59 rupees, but subsequently moved lower. \par \par \uc2  'Banks were a bit cautious to buy dollars in early trades but with the RBI delaying its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars at over the 36.65-rupee mark, the banks started aggressively bidding for the dollar,' a dealer at a private bank said. \par \par \uc2  He said the RBI is expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sell dollars at higher levels because dollar demand is expected to be stronger than dollar supply in the foreseeable future. \par \par \uc2  Last Friday, the dollar rose to close at 36.59 rupees, from 36.37 rupees a week earlier despite the RBI selling an estimated $650 million. \par \par \uc2  The dollar started rising sharply after the government raised oil prices last Monday, which is expected to raise dollar demand from oil companies, for their imports. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar is expected to range between 36.65 rupees and 36.70 rupees for the rest of the day Monday. \par \par \uc2  For the latest rupee indicative rates, please see Dow Jones Markets page [17960]. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 08-09-97 \par \par \uc2  0718GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 1847 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt9806tnn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
