\par\par \b \uc2  India rupee ranged vs spot dlr, touch up vs fwd. \b0\par\par\uc2 319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Sept 5 (Reuter) - The Indian rupee was range-bound at 36.54/56 per dollar in late morning spot deals and was stronger from its previous close in the forward market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said trade in the market was quieter compared to the volatility seen on Thursday when the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to arrest the rupee's fall. \par \par \uc2  The rupee opened at 35.54/56, weaker from Thursday's clsoe of 36.52/54. \par \par \uc2  "So far there has been no sign of the RBI," said a dealer at a foreign bank. "The market seems to be taking a breather before it makes a bid to break the 36.58 per dollar barrier." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said dollar demand from corporates remained good. \par \par \uc2  "There is latent demand for dollars," said a dealer at a private bank. "It appears that most of the banks are holding long dollar positions to the extent permitted by their overnight limits. This will have to get exhausted before any fresh action starts." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rupee was slightly up against the forward dollar. \par \par \uc2  March-delivery dollars were quoted in late morning at a premium of 1.62/65 rupees against Thursday's close of 1.65/66, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment is slightly better today on the newspaper report that RBI will allow oil firms to borrow from banks' foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits," said a dealer. \par \par \uc2  The Economic Times daily reported on Friday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had decided to allow banks to lend to oil firms from their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits. \par \par \uc2  Banks will also be allowed to swap such foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits directly with the RBI, the paper said. \par \par \uc2  "This has improved sentiment for now," said the private bank dealer. "But demand for forward cover remains good." \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay newsroom (91)-22-265 9000; Fax (91)-22-264 1699 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt9500568\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
