\par\par \b \uc2  India Strong Rupee Seen Chance For Liberalizing Imports \b0\par\par\uc2  By Timothy Mapes \par \uc2 227 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 1997\par 13:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI (AP-Dow Jones)--Stubborn strength in the Indian rupee could provide the government an opportunity to move quickly to dismantle its remaining barriers to imports and spur economic growth, but only if it can overcome daunting political obstacles, analysts and officials say. \par \par \uc2  While countries in South-East Asia have seen their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumble in recent weeks, Indian monetary officials have been faced with the opposite problem: a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  becoming so strong than many fear the country's exports will be priced out of world markets. \par \par \uc2  As foreign investment pours into the country, some of it apparently redirected from bleak markets elsewhere in Asia, the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been forced to repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to buy dollars to prevent the rupee from rising too far. That in turn has pushed money supply well above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's targeted level. \par \par \uc2  But some observers - including India's main trading partners - argue that this problem could be solved if the country opens its markets to more imports by scrapping remaining quotas and other restrictions. \par \par \uc2  'Given the level of inflows, one has to start taking these steps. Otherwise the rupee will remain highly overvalued,' said Anindya Chatterjee, an economist with Natwest Markets in Bombay. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 15-07-97 \par \par \uc2  1110GMT \par \par \uc2  1139 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt7f058bx\