\par\par \b \uc2  Reining Indian money supply growth seen tough job. \b0\par\par\uc2 493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, July 2 (Reuter) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a tough job trying to keep money supply within targets with foreign capital inflows continuing unabated and the government delaying a long-pending hike in petroleum prices, analysts said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) governor C. Rangarajan on Monday reiterated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's commitment to maintaining the money supply growth in the 15.0-15.5 percent target. \par \par \uc2  The M3 money supply growth rose to 16.6 percent on June 6 compared with 16.4 percent on May 23, according to latest figures released by the RBI. \par \par \uc2  "The key to both (controlling) money supply as well as rupee's depreciation is in the oil price hike," Chetan Ahya, analyst at Caspian Research said. \par \par \uc2  He said state-run oil firms have been raising offshore loans to pay for oil imports as the government has not reimbursed billions of rupees it owed these firms for the oil subsidy. \par \par \uc2  The oil price hike will raise revenues for oil firms who could then buy dollars from the local inter-bank market to fund imports, Ahya said. The resultant spurt in dollar demand will also help depreciate the overvalued Indian rupee, he added. \par \par \uc2  The RBI has been conducting regular repo auctions in recent months to mop up excess liquidity with banks but this is not sustainable over a long period, Anindya Chatterjee, economist at NatWest Markets, said. \par \par \uc2  Large capital inflows combined with RBI's dollar purchases from the market to arrest the rupee's appreciation have been fuelling money supply growth in recent months, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  India's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose to $24.88 billion on June 20, 1997, from $17.13 billion in June 1996. \par \par \uc2  Banks, flush with funds because of growing deposits and poor credit offtake, have been investing heavily in government securities and parking funds with RBI through repo auctions. \par \par \uc2  RBI's decision last week to cut the benchmark bank rate and the one-year deposit rate will also help to push down yields and reduce speculative inflows into the debt market, money market dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The RBI could control the money supply growth through several measures -- but each of them will be a difficult choice, French bank Societe Generale said in a \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  commentary. \par \par \uc2  It said the RBI could suspend its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but it would not be in the interest of Indian exporters. \par \par \uc2  The government could issue fresh bonds to mop up liquidity, but over 50 percent of the budgeted borrowing programme is already over in the first quarter of 1996/97 (April-March). \par \par \uc2  The RBI could impose cash reserve requirement on expatriate Indians' foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated deposits, the bank said. \par \par \uc2  This may lead to a slowdown in expatriate Indians' deposits but such a move will not be welcomed by overseas Indians or banks, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay newsroom +91-22-265 9000 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt720344w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
