\par\par \b \uc2  India seen facing macro maze on convertible rupee. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Simon Cameron-Moore \par \uc2 803 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, June 4 (Reuter) - India's plan for a fully convertible rupee by the turn of the century poses the problem of stopping an overvalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  getting stronger, analysts said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Rajan Govil, economist at HSBC B & K, reckons the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) will have to engineer a weaker rupee soon, helped by a slowdown in capital inflows. \par \par \uc2  "Exports were down 10 percent in April and and the strong rupee is a major reason," he said. \par \par \uc2  He expected the RBI to drag the rupee back to 37.8 to the dollar by March 1998, compared to Wednesday's rate of 35.77. \par \par \uc2  But India will pay for its more competitive exchange rate with higher inflation, Govil said. \par \par \uc2  A committee headed by former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor S.S. Tarapore, which on Tuesday presented a report commissioned by Finance Minister P. Chidamabaram on how to make the rupee convertible on the capital account, was quite clear on the exchange rate risks involved. \par \par \uc2  "The international experience has shown that liberalisation of the capital account induces large capital inflows, which can cause a real appreciation of the exchange rate and erode the effectiveness of certain domestic macroeconomic policies," the report said. \par \par \uc2  The committee recommended that an inflation rate of three to five percent was one prerequisite for full convertibility. \par \par \uc2  It also said the Reserve Bank of India should be given more independence to pursue this goal. \par \par \uc2  Deregulation of interest rates and a reduction in banks' cash reserve ratio (CRR) to three percent by 1999/2000 from the current 9.5 percent were also on the committee's wish list. \par \par \uc2  Somehow the RBI must balance these goals with managing the exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  Most analysts reckon the rupee should be shedding three to five percent of its value annually, based on purchasing power parity criteria which take account of inflation differentials between India and major convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  And Chidambaram, while leaving the RBI to do the job, said last month he would not like the rupee to strengthen further. \par \par \uc2  The RBI has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to stop the rupee appreciating. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, including gold and special drawing rights, have shot up to $27.79 billion, covering seven months' imports, from $21.6 billion a year ago -- which is a positive by-product for a country pursuing convertibility. \par \par \uc2  But the sale of rupees for dollars increases the domestic money supply, which will lead to inflation unless the RBI sterilises the surplus rupees through sales of government debt. \par \par \uc2  That is exactly what the RBI has done. \par \par \uc2  It has sold government paper covering around one-third of the central government's 1997/98 borrowing requirement in the first two months of the fiscal year. \par \par \uc2  Economists wonder what the RBI will do if the rupee is still straining at the leash at the end of the year, when there is little debt left to sell. \par \par \uc2  The authorities will be left with a stark choice of either letting the rupee rise or allowing inflation to rise, Sanjeev Sanyal, analyst at Bombay brokerage SocGen-Crosby, said. \par \par \uc2  "We're expecting the wholesale price index to average eight percent in 1997/98 against 6.4 percent last year," said HSBC's Govil. The WPI is running at 6.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Tarapore committee has pitched for an ideal, where all targets are achieved and the rupee can make a soft landing into the world of full convertibility. \par \par \uc2  But analysts are sceptical that the macroeconomic targets can be achieved. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at a major U.S. investment bank said India should press ahead with convertibility even if the targets are missed, as the markets could then force the monetary and fiscal discipline wherever the authorities fail. \par \par \uc2  "If you do not have a credible policy you should go ahead with capital convertibility because the markets will force one on you," he said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has remained stubbornly strong, backed by heavy capital inflows, against the dollar, which itself is strong against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. banker said the rupee is currently overvalued by 10 percent on its real effective exchange rate index (REER) using 1993 as base year. \par \par \uc2  The Tarapore committee has recommended that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  try to hold the rupee in a plus or minus five percent band on its REER index. \par \par \uc2  It also said the index should be published to provide the market with a guide to the authorities' intentions. Currently, only an out of date REER index is published by the RBI, which releases the data infrequently. \par \par \uc2  "I think there is a real risk. You have to sterilise that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The RBI has to resolve what it wants to target - money supply or REER," the U.S. investment banker said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt6404kpy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
