\par\par \b \uc2  India: Rupee Steady Midday; Mkt Ponders RBI's Intentions \b0\par\par\uc2 231 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 1997\par 09:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee is steady Thursday against the U.S. dollar in Bombay interbank trading, as players ponder whether the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to purchase the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is quoted between 35.815 rupees and 35.825 rupees with very few trades, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened between 35.82 rupees and 35.83 rupees, then slipped in early trading to between 35.815 rupees and 35.82 rupees, dealers said. It closed Wednesday around 35.825 rupees. \par \par \uc2  'I don't think the RBI will come in today (Thursday) to buy dollars,' a dealer at a state-owned bank said. 'They will start buying only if the dollar falls below 35.80 rupees.' \par \par \uc2  The RBI hasn't been seen in the spot market for the last two days, dealers said. They said, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars for delivery at the end of the month in late afternoon trading Wednesday and that pushed the forward premiums up slightly early Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The RBI has been buying dollars for rupees to keep the rupee from appreciating against the dollar and hurting Indian exports. \par \par \uc2  It bought an estimated $450 million last week and an estimated $30 million to $50 million Monday. \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES INDIA REPORT  15-05-97 \par \par \uc2  0722GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 1847 62506 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020011004dt5f00pd7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
