\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee Steady Early Despite Selling Pressure On Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2 402 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 1997\par 06:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee opened little changed against the U.S. dollar in a thin market Wednesday as players sought to push the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower in an effort to test the market's downside, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  'There was a bit of selling pressure on the dollar, but not much business was transacted,' a dealer at a state-owned bank said. Only a couple of small trades were reported, following the first 10 minutes of dealing, which saw no trades at all, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar-selling, however, was cautious, because most players fear the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the rupee from appreciating against the dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened between 35.83 rupees and 35.84 rupees and is currently trading between 35.835 rupees and 35.840 rupees, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed Tuesday between 35.835 rupees and 35.840 rupees. \par \par \uc2  'I think the dollar should bottom out around 35.825 rupees,' the chief dealer at a private sector bank said. 'If it gets buying support from the Reserve Bank (of India) or the State Bank, it will top around 35.865 rupees.' \par \par \uc2  State Bank of India is the country's largest commercial bank and it trades in the market on behalf of several large companies, including state-owned oil companies. Its dollar purchases Tuesday pushed the dollar up from its intraday low. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the RBI was unlikely to buy dollars Wednesday, because current exchange-rate levels are thought to be within the range viewed as acceptable to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  'The RBI will step in only if the dollar falls below 35.83 rupees,' a dealer at a foreign bank said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't seen in the market Tuesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The RBI is estimated to have bought $450 million last week and an estimated $30 million to $50 million Monday. Those purchases were aimed primarily at setting a floor for the dollar against the rupee, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In the fiscal year ended March 31, the RBI is reported to have bought $7.8 billion to keep the rupee from appreciating against the dollar and thereby hurting India's exports. Of this, $1 billion worth of purchases are estimated to have been made in March alone. \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES INDIA REPORT  14-05-97 \par \par \uc2  0418GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 1847 62506 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020011004dt5e00oid\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
