\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee ends up vs dlr on large capital flows. \b0\par\par\uc2 192 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, April 15 (Reuter) - The Indian rupee ended up against the dollar on Tuesday on the back of large dollar sale by exporters and good inward remittances, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers have of late said they noticed a reluctance on the part of the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for fear of its impact on the domestic money market. \par \par \uc2  "RBI did not buy at earlier levels today," said a dealer, referring to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's earlier dollar purchases at 35.84/85 levels. \par \par \uc2  RBI bought at 35.81/82 rupees to a dollar on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The rupee rose on the forward dollar market, reflecting RBI's initiatives in its 1997/98 (April-March) credit policy to prompt a cut in banks' lending rates. \par \par \uc2  "It was by and large discounted, but still had its impact," said a dealer at a foreign bank in Bombay. \par \par \uc2  The six month annualised forward premium closed Tuesday at 5.75 percent compared with the previous close of 6.19 percent. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay newsroom +91-22-265 9000 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt4f0734d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
