\par\par \b \uc2  Indian Rupee Stronger On Heavy Dlr Sales Ahead Of Holiday \b0\par\par\uc2 182 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 1996\par 15:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI (AP-Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee strengthened Wednesday on significant dollar selling due to technical reasons ahead of a holiday Monday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said demand for dollars trailed behind supply, and the dollar fell continuously from opening levels of around 34.21 rupees. \par \par \uc2  'It was a one-way market, and the way was down,' a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  At the 1030 GMT close of Bombay inter-bank trading, the dollar was trading around 34.16 rupees, down from 34.21 rupees in late trading Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  One trader said the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar by buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from banks during the day, but other traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't spotted in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  'It was mainly technical selling. There was no need for the RBI really,' a trader with a U.S. bank said. \par \par \uc2  RBI officials weren't available to confirm the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's intevention. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 24-04-96 \par \par \uc2  1357GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds4o03h7p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
