\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee Rises Sharply, But RBI Dlr Buying Limits Gains \b0\par\par\uc2 245 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1996\par 12:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI (AP-Dow Jones)--Strong dollar inflows gave a strong boost to the Indian rupee Monday, but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) managed to hold the rupee within recent trading ranges, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In active trading, dealers noted that the dollar opened around 34.55 rupees, unchanged from Friday, but then fell steadily through the day as several state banks and financial institutions unloaded holdings of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The sales pushed the dollar below a key level at 34.40 rupees, triggering further stop-loss sales that extended the move down to 34.10 rupees by late afternoon trading. \par \par \uc2  'Basically it was a one-way street,' a trader at a foreign bank in Bombay said, adding that the dollar supplies seemed tied to some foreign portfolio investment and some debt issues by Indian companies abroad. \par \par \uc2  But the Reserve Bank blocked the dollar's slide at 34.10 rupees, buying dollars from several banks at levels between 34.10 rupees and 34.15 rupees, traders who dealt with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  A spokeswoman for the RBI confirmed that the RBI had bought dollars, but as usual declined to comment on the levels or the size of the transactions. \par \par \uc2  In late trading Monday, the dollar was quoted at 34.13 rupees, down from 34.55 rupees late Friday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 25-03-96 \par \par \uc2  CORRECTED 0708 ET \par \par \uc2  1164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds3p02m47\par }\page {\pa