\par\par \b \uc2  Indian Rupee Strengthens After Early Drop \b0\par\par\uc2 236 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1996\par 07:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI (AP-Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee rebounded after an early drop Thursday, as traders sold off long positions in the dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted that the dollar opened around 35.40 rupees, and then rose as high as 35.59 rupees in early trading. \par \par \uc2  But after hitting that peak, the market reversed course as players tried to unload their dollar holdings, and the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped back to 35.20 rupees at 0615 GMT. Late Wednesday, the dollar traded at 35.35 rupees in a holiday-thinned market, as banks in Bombay and Madras were closed. \par \par \uc2  'A lot of people sold. They were trying to get out of their long positions,' a trader at a foreign bank in Bombay said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also reported some rumors of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  near the dollar's peak Thursday. But traders that typically deal with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that it hasn't operated in the market so far Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders added, however, that commercial demand for the dollar remains fairly strong, and predicted that the dollar could push back up later in the day. \par \par \uc2  'I think it holds here and then goes back up,' the foreign bank trader said, adding that 35.00 rupees should be a solid floor for the dollar at the moment. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 21-03-96 \par \par \uc2  0622GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds3l02gh4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pic