\par\par \b \uc2  India RBI says buying dollars to stabilise rupee. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shyam Kumar \par \uc2 553 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, March 6 (Reuter) - The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stabilise the rupee's exchange value, an RBI official told Reuters on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Why should people say we're buying dollars to recoup our reserves?" asked a RBI official, who declined to be identified. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to stabilise the exchange rate." \par \par \uc2  The official was referring to comments that RBI could be buying dollars to replenish its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets. Another explanation was that RBI could be buying dollars against rupees to inject liquidity in a tight domestic money market. \par \par \uc2  According to official figures, India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have fallen to $20.65 billion as on February 23, 1996 from $23.95 billion a year ago. \par \par \uc2  Following its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by selling dollars after the rupee began a free fall in mid-1995, the RBI has started selling dollars since last week. \par \par \uc2  It bought an estimated $10 million dollars each on Friday and Monday and on Wednesday, purchased around $15 million, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The RBI does not comment on the quantum of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Any large-scale buying of dollars would help replenish the reserves for battles ahead while adding to the domestic rupee availability," ICICI Securities said in a commentary. \par \par \uc2  It said rising forward premia on the dollar and the falling spot rate are at a divergence that suggested a weakness in the current rally. \par \par \uc2  "The trade deficit in January against expectations of slight surplus, large oil payments ahead due to rising consumption and oil prices and subtle signs of a slowdown in FII (foreign institutional investor) buying could portend tough times ahead for the rupee," it said. \par \par \uc2  But it added that a revival in the GDR (global depositary receipts) markets and sustained FII buying would render most supply-deficit calculations meaningless and reverse the role of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to capping the gains of the rupee, it said. \par \par \uc2  Treasury managers with banks said the relatively firm forward premia on dollar at 19 percent did not reflect genuine demand for dollars from importers. \par \par \uc2  "Importer-covering has virtually stopped since February 5," said a leading dealer. \par \par \uc2  He said the high forward premia reflected the high swap costs incurred by private and foreign banks which are very dependent on expatriate FCNR (foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  non-resident) deposits. \par \par \uc2  "High forward premia is the result of compulsions of bank treasuries to raise rupee resources," said the vice president, treasury at a private Indian bank. \par \par \uc2  The high forward premia would continue as long as the liquidity crunch in the domestic money market continued, forcing banks to convert their dollar assets to rupees at high cost, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Indian rupee started falling against the dollar in mid-1995 after remaining steady at 31.37 for almost two years. \par \par \uc2  In the first week of February, the rupee touched a historic low of 38.35 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has revived suddenly during the last two weeks on the back of remittanaces from foreign institutional investors and Indian firms which raised equity from overseas markets. \par \par \uc2  A slow-down in importer covering aided the rupee's rally. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 34.02/12 in latest deals. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay newsroom +91-22-285 2851 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds3600rme\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
