\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee Continues To Rise But RBI Trims Gains \b0\par\par\uc2 214 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1996\par 12:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI (AP-Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee continued its recent rally against the dollar Friday, although \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back from intraday highs. \par \par \uc2  Traders reported heavy dollar selling early in the day, pushing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down to an intraday low around 34.25 rupees. \par \par \uc2  But some demand from commercial banks helped the dollar rebound from that level, and the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to rise after the Reserve Bank stepped in to buy small lots of dollars from a few banks in mid-afternoon trading. \par \par \uc2  Traders were somewhat surprised by the move, having generally discounted the possibility of RBI action earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  But they said the relatively small amounts purchased probably indicate that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  simply wants to calm things down, rather than push the dollar higher right away. \par \par \uc2  They note that the dollar has fallen by some 10% against the rupees over the last month, after gaining about the same amount during a rally in January. \par \par \uc2  'The situation has turned around completely,' said Lester Pereira, chief dealer at Barclays Bank in Bombay. 'They want to see stability,' he said. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 01-03-96 \par \par \uc2  1121GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds3101uop\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley10