\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee Rallies Vs Dlr On Continuing Inflows \b0\par\par\uc2 246 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 1996\par 15:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI (AP-Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee continued its recent rally Wednesday, rising to a two-month high against the dollar on continuing inflows from foreign investors and selling by Indian exporting companies. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that the dollar moved in a wide range Wednesday, opening around 35.60 rupees, and then falling to a low 35.08 rupees at midday. Some short covering boosted the dollar briefly at that point, but the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  settled back to 35.15 rupees by late trading. \par \par \uc2  'It was a strong rally,' a trader at a foreign bank in Bombay said, noting that dollar selling seemed widespread from a number of sources, including foreign investors and exporting companies. \par \par \uc2  In the forward market, however, forward premiums for dollars continued to rise, despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said some sales of dollars for March delivery by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did little to arrest the climb in forward premiums. The forward premium for March rose to 1.20 rupees Wednesday, versus 1.05 rupees Tuesday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Traders added that the announcement by the government of an interim budget had no impact on dealings Wednesday, as it came after trading had ended for the day. And they added that the lack of any market-specific moves would mean the market would largely ignore the budget when trading continues Thursday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 28-02-96 \par \par \uc2  1408GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020